-- gap[100] rung 4 — authority is an OPERAND, and the subject is the VALUE.
--
-- CORRECTED 2026-08-09. The first version of this proof was wrong in exactly
-- the way the owner directive names: it wrote `elapsed(realclock, since)`,
-- putting the WORLD first. Under FACE-CALL ("if you are holding the first
-- argument, you are holding the receiver") that makes the capability container
-- the subject — which is pseudo-OOP smuggled in through a capability argument,
-- and it is not what the law says.
--
-- The correct decomposition separates three things the first version fused:
--
--   subject   the VALUE the relation is about        (the timestamp, the path)
--   context   the AUTHORITY required to perform it   (the clock, the filesystem)
--   relation  the operation itself                   (elapsed, read)
--
-- So the surface follows the SEMANTIC SUBJECT and the world rides as an
-- ordinary operand: `since:elapsed(w)`, not `w:elapsed(since)`. For the
-- filesystem the same reading gives `path:read(fs)` rather than
-- `fs:read(path)` — the path is what the relation is ABOUT; the filesystem is
-- what makes performing it permissible.
--
-- "Declare from the relation; work from the value." A world is an independent
-- semantic operand, not a receiver merely because it grants authority.
--
-- WHAT THIS STILL PROVES, unchanged and the only thing that matters: authority
-- can be WITHHELD. A world is only a world if handing over a different one
-- changes what the callee can do. Asserting the happy path alone proves
-- nothing — an ambient call passes that too.
--
-- exit 0 all good; 1..3 names the direction that broke.

realclock = { now = () os.clock() }

-- Same shape, no authority behind it. This is what "refused" looks like from
-- the callee's side.
fakeclock = { now = () 0.0 }

-- The SUBJECT is the timestamp. The world is a named operand the caller had to
-- be handed and could have been refused. A reader sees the authority this
-- relation takes without reading its body — the property ambient reach destroys.
-- NOTE: the subject is a NAMED parameter, not the ambient `.`. SELF-ZERO says
-- the receiver is spelled `.` and the parameter list carries TRUE ARGUMENTS
-- ONLY, so the canonical declaration is `elapsed = (w) (w.now() - .)`. Measured
-- 2026-08-09: that does not parse — `. * 2.0` fails with ExpectedToken. The
-- ambient-subject form is law and is unimplemented, so this proof uses a named
-- subject. The ORIENTATION being proven is unaffected: the subject is still the
-- value and the world is still an operand.
elapsed = (since: f64, w): f64
    w.now() - since

main = (): i64
    -- direction 1: the real world works, subject-first. The baseline is a
    -- literal rather than realclock.now() because binding a world-member call
    -- result to a typed local does not lower today (see the note below).
    a = 0.0
    d = a:elapsed(realclock)
    if d < 0.0
        print("world proof: real clock ran backwards: {d}")
        std.os.exit(1)

    -- direction 2: THE CAPABILITY CAN BE WITHHELD. Same subject, same relation,
    -- a world with no authority — and the answer changes.
    base = 1000.0
    z = base:elapsed(fakeclock)
    if z != -1000.0
        print("world proof: withheld clock still reached real time: {z}")
        std.os.exit(2)

    -- direction 3: the operand is not decorative. A callee that took the world
    -- and then reached os.clock() anyway would answer the same for both.
    zero = 0.0
    if zero:elapsed(realclock) == zero:elapsed(fakeclock)
        print("world proof: both worlds gave one answer — the operand is decorative")
        std.os.exit(3)

    print("world proof: subject-first, authority as an operand, and it CAN be withheld")
    0
